-- SHADOW-SEMANTICS cell I18: an interface reached ONLY through a module ALIAS.
--
-- CONFORMANT under S1-NS (RULED 2026-08-08; docs/spec/SHADOW-SEMANTICS.md, the "S1-NS" block
-- under S1). `size` IS in S1's right operand here, and 7 is the correct, conformant answer --
-- not a known-inconsistent placeholder awaiting a fix.
--
-- S1-NS (a) is a UNION over three arms; a bare method name is admitted if ANY ONE of them
-- holds. Arm (i), the TYPE arm, does fail exactly as this row used to argue: `import smod as S`
-- cannot put a TYPE name in scope -- an alias-qualified name in type position is a parse error
-- -- and `impl Sizeable Blob` in this module is correctly rejected `Unknown interface:
-- Sizeable`. But arm (ii), the VALUE arm, is satisfied independently: it admits `size` via "a
-- member list naming `n`, a wildcard, or a module alias (`import ifc as A`, since `A.n` is a
-- legal call)" -- and `import smod as S` is exactly that alias. Because (a) is a union, arm
-- (i)'s failure does not disqualify the row: `size` is an importer shadow in this module, S2's
-- importer-shadow rule finds a live `impl Sizeable String` at the receiver's head, and dispatch
-- to the method -- 7 -- is what conformance requires.
--
-- MECHANISM, KEPT FOR THE RECORD (real, and still load-bearing elsewhere -- not the reason 7 is
-- right here, since S1-NS settles that without needing it): `renameAliasedMethods` rewrites
-- `S.size` to a BARE `size` before the mark pass, so an alias-qualified call and a bare
-- reference to an explicitly-imported standalone become the SAME token by the time any rule
-- here runs -- see `both.mdk` beside this file, which puts both spellings in one module and
-- shows `run` cannot tell them apart. This is why an import-clause-shaped rule can never
-- recover which spelling was written: the qualifier is gone by marking time, so any fix
-- attempted at that layer reasons from information that no longer exists. It was tried once
-- anyway, conditioning admission on "names this module otherwise binds": it produced `run` = 99
-- against a built binary of 7, at exit 0 with no diagnostic -- a NEW silent divergence, worse
-- than the inconsistency it was trying to remove. This row does not need that fix -- S1-NS
-- answers it directly, over names, without touching provenance -- but the erasure it documents
-- is real, which is why `both.mdk` is graded as its own row rather than assumed to follow from
-- this one.
import fmod.{size}
import smod as S

main = println (size "abc")
